							</td>
							<td width="75%" align="left">
								&nbsp;<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php#1">General Category</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?board=42.0">The Otherquests</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1637.0#msg17948">Some thoughts and ramblings</a>
							</td>
							<td align="right" style="padding: 0 1ex; white-space: nowrap;">
								on: September 26, 2005, 09:48:15 am
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">Class Balance. <br /><br />Always seems to be a trigger for drama, protests in game, on forums, along with angst filled posts... I am guilty of some of that as well, and still tend to lament the state of the Ranger in EQ to this day.<br /><br />No game seems to be able to escape this dreaded spectre of doom, some have it a bit tougher than others however. Everquest always had some complaints of poor class balance for years, but when they added two move over the life of the game, it also brought about even higher levels of drama, and angst.<br /><br />Beastlords eventually earned a nitch, but Berzerkers are still regarded by the majority of EQ players as just a foot note, and in many player&#039;s eyes could be removed from game tomorrow, and no one would really notice.<br /><br />The way Beastlords eventually gained acceptance was with the addition of the Spirit lines, that added group Mana/HP regen, and the Paragon Alternate Advancment skills, which many raid leaders eventually realized is a strong tool in encounters. Paragon coupled with Mass Group Buff Alternate Advancemnt skills could be a raid maker/breaker if done correctly.<br /><br />Berzerkers will have it harder, since they are technically more of a melee class etc.... and with Melee&#039;s have not being known as Buffing classes they will have to compensate/change the class into some kind of god mode for at least a period of time before many people would even think of touching the class. <br /><br />There are more factors to it than skills though, the length of time it takes to level a character in Everquest is also a deterant for new classes to gain a foothold.<br /><br />Sure some crazy fanatic can level a toon in 1 week, and have a level 70 bot... but they won&#039;t have casting, or melee skills worth a damn. And in Everquest that is a bad thing. <br /><br />But this all goes alot deeper than just adding two more classes over a couple years. THe root issues were always there, the new classes just made them more pronounced.<br /><br />In most MMORPG&#039;s, you have a tank, healer, DPS, and sometimes debuffing/CC classes. 4 Basic roles. The problems come when you take the absolute pure classes:<br /><br />Healer:&nbsp; EQ - Cleric :: WoW - Priest<br /><br />Tank: EQ - Warrior :: WoW - Warrior<br /><br />DPS: EQ - Rogue, Wizard, Monk, Berzerker, Necromancer, Magicians&nbsp; :: WoW - Rogue, Hunter, Warlock<br /><br />Debuffing/CC: EQ - Bard, Enchanter, Shaman :: WoW - Mage Humanoid/Beast CC and Priest on Undead, all single target CC, and most classes have some forms of Debuff, so the lines are very blurred there.<br /><br />Then you have the hybrid types in both games.<br /><br />EQ Hybrids: Ranger, Beastlord, Shadowknight, Paladin, Bards (in many ways the ultimate hybrid) Druids (Yeah, I know they are technically a pure priest caster, but thier skills, and spells are so generalized, with some debuffs, nukes, and heals, that you can&#039;t really consider them pure in any line of thought) <br /><br />WoW Hybrids would consist of Paladin (Alliance Only,) Shaman (Horde Only,) <br /><br />It can actually get much more complicated than that though. For example, in EQ, a Warrior is a meat shield, that&#039;s it, nothing more. In WoW, with the way talents were created in the game, you can be that perfect meatshield once again, or a kick ass DPS class (but a mediocre tank at best.) <br /><br />Rogues in both games are also about the same in general, they both stealth, get bonuses in DPS when coming out of stealth at times, and can use stealth to escape nasty situations.<br /><br />Priests and Clerics in both games are close to the same in many ways, except that Clerics in EQ are never ever allowed to do any reasonable form of DPS, while a Priest in WoW can choose to be a more DPS minded class, or pure healer. A Shadow Spec&#039;d priest in WoW can be pretty solid DPS. A cleric in EQ no matter how many AA&#039;s they pick up, will never really be considered DPS. Of course, a DPS spec&#039;d priest in WoW, is a mediocre healer as well.<br /><br />I think this also displays a big difference between the two games, and thier concepts of how to build classes.<br /><br />EQ has Alternate Advancments, and you can continually gain points, and eventually fill out every single one of them.<br /><br />WoW has a Talents System, and there are typically 3 trees, and the pontential that if you had enough points you could most likely spend 100 points to fill them all up, but (and there&#039;s always one of those huh?) They give you one talent point per level past 10, for a grand total available of 51 talent points you can spend any way you like....<br /><br />What does that mean? It means that in EQ all classes will eventually be cookie cutters of one another.<br /><br />Classes in WoW can be side by side, and be totally different in what they can do.<br /><br />Take for instance my Warrior in WoW, named Zigurn, level 58 now, and a mixture of Arms, Protection, and Fury Talent Spec&#039;s. I wanted a good instance tank, so I am building him with many of the protection talents, with anything that will help generate aggro, or protect him some from Fury, and Arms. In general, he tanks much better than many warriors at level 60, who are Fury Spec&#039;d. Quite fun actually. But should I ever want to PvP with him, he&#039;s gonna be a big fat lump and an easy target for some DPS minded classes/builds.<br /><br />But in general, both games need a tank, healer, and DPS to accomplish the goals in the game. Those &quot;pure&quot; classes get pissy when they see someone edging onto thier &quot;turf&quot;, and cry and whine.<br /><br />I think next generation MMO&#039;s need to drop that strict model, and come up with a system, and events that are less dependant on single classes to fill specific roles, and allow more hybridization at all levels. Don&#039;t have any pure DPS, pure Tanking, Pure healing etc... make it so that each class has certain niches that make them special, but allow them to also cross lines as needed.</td>
						</tr>
