								&nbsp;<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php#1">General Category</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?board=2.0">Non-EQ (Hey Real Life!)</a> / <a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1634.15#msg17893">Re: Something to think about</a>
							</td>
							<td align="right" style="padding: 0 1ex; white-space: nowrap;">
								on: September 08, 2005, 01:25:33 pm
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">Amazing that some people have no concept of what it takes to really strengthen the levees in an area like that... <br /><br />There are miles and miles of levees, and no amount of simple sandbagging could have prevented this. The Levee system in New Orleans has been a subject of debate for at least as long as I can remember, at 33 years of age... SOme of them date back originally to 1718.<br /><br />They had maybe 3 days warning that the storm was looking to hit them?<br /><br />That&#039;s the real god damned problem in this country. We all expect instant gratification in our society in all aspects, and many people expect entitlements to said gratifications. It would take weeks to get enough materials and manpower into the city to ahve reinforced those levees strong enough to withstand that storm. <br /><br />The simple reality is not one thing could have been done to make this less of a disaster in the time frame that the storm was active, and growing. <br /><br />What&#039;s even more ironic, is if someone really wants to point fingers, thier own mayer, and city government should have been listening to scientists, and experts who have warned of this exact scenario for many many years, yet they pooh poohed the whole thing, saying &quot;what are the chances?&quot; or it would cost too much.<br /><br />Well... here&#039;s a news flash for that ass Nagin, because his city never took the time to protect itself for such a circumstance, it will now pay a much higher price, not only in dollars, but also in the lives that have been lost in the process. <br /><br />It is not the federal government&#039;s fault in this case. <br /><br />Let&#039;s also consider the scale of what we are talking about, this was a city of what, half a million people? Has anyone even thought about the amount of housing, logistics, and other aspects to dealing with just such a catastrophy? <br /><br />Nagin bitching about the Army Corps of Engineers not getting those breaches plugged faster shows what a jackass this guy is. We&#039;re talking levee breaches 2-3 city fucking blocks long for gods sake, with no roads to get to them, and no materials in place to even begin to plug them. <br /><br />Does anyone know the amount of force rushing water has in these situations? Boulders get moved in heavy rapids... so a 3000 pound sandbag is shit in comparison. <br /><br />People need to sit the fuck back and really grasp the scope of what has occured before they lay out claims of racisim, and letting black people die etc... That Bush is just ignoring it because it was mostly black people. For fucks sake... that is so totally ignorant.<br /><br />Like when CNN broadcast people sitting on thier fucking asses chanting &quot;We want help! We want help! We want help!&quot; uhm.... lets see... I saw few crippled people... there is such a thing as you know... walking? But they wait on busses... they expect the federal handout now more than ever... and that is a truely sad sign in our society today.</td>
