						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1621.msg17787#msg17787">Quote from: Shrek on July 30, 2005, 02:23:30 am</a></div><div class="quote">Look before you stick that foot in your mouth?<br /><br />WoW - 2 million subscribers in early June, they didn&#039;t release in China&nbsp; and gain the other 1.5 mil people till June 7th, and the numbers weren&#039;t realeased right away.<br /></div><br />This is open to debate, but I actually think the numbers for WoW are a bit inflated atm as well.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1621.msg17787#msg17787">Quote from: Shrek on July 30, 2005, 02:23:30 am</a></div><div class="quote">EQ2 - Announced in Jan 2005 they broke 300k subscribers.&nbsp; They even had the add slogan, we did in three months what took EQ three years...<br /></div><br />And they have been interestingly quiet about any new numbers since January. They certainly aren&#039;t bragging openly about how big a success it is anymore. And it&#039;s 6 months later, and he shows them still going up for subscribers? Based on public opinion, EQ2 is not well liked in the genre in general.<br /><br />I also take issue with the fact that with shared subscribtions between EQ/EQ2/SWG on the all access pass, how do those affect any published numbers that SoE would release?<br /><br />The whole EQ/EQ2 subscriber numbers are one of the biggest reasons I think this guy&#039;s chart is totally innaccurate.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1621.msg17787#msg17787">Quote from: Shrek on July 30, 2005, 02:23:30 am</a></div><div class="quote">Lineage - If you don&#039;t know how big of a deal Lineage was/is you obviously didn&#039;t read much, or know any Koreans in EQ.<br /></div><br />I never said I doubted it&#039;s popularity in general, I just questioned the total subscriber numbers as seeming a bit high. It&#039;s beaten EQ hands down for total numbers playing it for ages, and has been a well accepted fact in that regard.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1621.msg17787#msg17787">Quote from: Shrek on July 30, 2005, 02:23:30 am</a></div><div class="quote">EQ - No idea personally, but considering he&#039;s right on everything else I doubt he&#039;s lying.&nbsp; Plus he&#039;s talking paid accounts not active players.&nbsp; <br /><br />He&#039;s been doing it for a few years now, it&#039;s not like his site is new.&nbsp; And I&#039;ve never seen him called on the carpet as being wrong either.<br /><br />You&#039;re welcome to your opinon, but your examples of him being wrong are.... um... wrong =P<br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote"><br />And just like my opinions, yours can just as well be called into question. Because they, just like this jaskass&#039;s chart, are nothing but opinions, and guesses.<br /><br />This isn&#039;t like sales data that can be gathered from different sources and compiled for charting sales of a one person shooter style of game etc... Like when Wal-Mart might publish how well a game did etc... in weekend sales on release, and then when comparing other seller&#039;s numbers, you can get a real idea of market saturation.<br /><br />Since the subscriber numbers are wholely an internally known number, and rarely given out in public when things start to slide down the slope of poor performance, his numbers for some of the games listed is rose colored glasses at best.<br /><br />There&#039;s also been no answers ever from SoE on how they treat the all access passes. Do those mean that they count them as a valid subscription for all games in the pass? Even if the person who has it only regularly plays EQ and SWG for instance, does that also get reflected as an EQ2 subscription as well? In theory you could fairly say this would be justified, but when it comes down to actual player base numbers, it makes it a totally false number though.<br /><br />There&#039;s too little solid reportable information in this market segment for anyone to accurately make this type of chart, and anyone who beleives what this type of chart says as gospel, is only foolling themselves.<br /><br />I&#039;m a die hard cycnic when it comes to corporations, and how the report data, it will always take a spin to the most positive, and since subscriber numbers can be taken as a sign of how successfull a game is in this arena, few if any companies will release exact data on thier subscriber base when business starts going south.<br /><br />A fair indicator that a game is hurting for actual subscribers?<br /><br />When they continually email people to come back for a free month etc etc etc... Is a really good example.</div></div></td>
